29,2,1,talk,Toshio,,"It appears that projects benefiting a single wing are poorly received, while shared spaces are highly valued. Additionally, ground-floor work in older blocks is frequently delayed by hazardous materials.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
30,2,1,talk,Victoria,,"My findings show a significant imbalance; specialist rooms are often idle, while general-purpose rooms are overbooked. Furthermore, taking rooms out of service has forced the division to pay for expensive off-site hired space.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
31,2,1,talk,George,,"I have found that our front-of-house and grounds posts remain unfilled. More concerningly, our bench technicians are spending hours on manual routines that the current equipment simply cannot handle.","{""words"":29,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
32,2,1,talk,Mizuki,,"The delivery schedules show a clear pattern: projects based on comfort or appeal often slip by a year or more, whereas those addressing inspection findings are delivered on time. We must be wary of optimistic timelines.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
33,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My research suggests a worrying gap between planning and reality. Mechanical and plant costs are climbing sharply, yet we have no renewal reserve for aging audiovisual kit. We are essentially planning for a future we cannot afford to maintain.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
34,2,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Victoria, if general rooms are overbooked, a shared-use facility would solve that while ensuring high user satisfaction. However, we must avoid the ground floors of the older blocks to prevent delays.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
35,2,2,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, I agree. A shared-use facility would be efficient, but we must consider the disruption. If we take rooms out of service, we risk further expensive off-site hiring. We need a plan that minimizes timetable displacement.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,2,talk,George,,"@Victoria, minimizing displacement is wise, but we must also consider the human cost. If we implement a plan that adds to the manual burden of our already stretched technicians, it will be unsustainable.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I agree. We cannot ignore the staff's capacity. Furthermore, we must avoid any project that relies on ""appeal"" or ""comfort"" as its primary driver, as those are the ones most prone to severe schedule slippage.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mizuki, your observation on ""appeal"" is quite poignant. It seems we are often seduced by the vision while ignoring the invoice. If we choose a project with complex mechanical needs, we must account for those rising costs now, not later.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,3,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, the financial risk is clear. If we combine your cost concerns with my findings on hazardous materials, any ground-floor renovation in the old blocks is a liability. We need a project that is functional and shared.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,3,talk,Victoria,,"@Toshio, I concur. A functional, shared-use project is the most rational path, provided it doesn't trigger the off-site hiring costs I discovered. We must prioritize a plan that balances high demand with minimal timetable disruption.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,3,talk,George,,"I agree with the need for functionality, but I must insist that we prioritize automation or equipment upgrades. Our technicians are already overburdened with manual routines; we cannot add more to their plates.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I understand your concern for the staff. However, we must also consider the physical environment. My research into site diaries shows significant time lost to weather; any project with extensive outdoor work is a risk to the calendar.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mizuki, a charming thought, but nature is rarely as predictable as a spreadsheet. Between the weather and the rising cost of plant lots, an ambitious outdoor expansion seems like a recipe for a budget disaster.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,4,talk,Toshio,,"It seems we have a consensus on the risks. We must avoid ground-floor work in old blocks, outdoor expansions, and projects that increase manual labor or rely on ""appeal."" A shared, indoor, equipment-focused facility is the safest bet.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,4,talk,Victoria,,"I agree with that summary. To be truly sustainable, the project must also ensure that the rooms created are actually needed and that the construction phase doesn't cripple our current timetable. Efficiency in use is as vital as efficiency in cost.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,4,talk,George,,"I concur. As long as the equipment focus includes automating those tedious manual routines, we can support our staff while meeting the university's needs. A project that reduces the burden on our technicians is the only responsible choice.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,4,talk,Mizuki,,"I agree. We need a project that is logically grounded in necessity rather than desire. If it is indoor, equipment-focused, and addresses a critical gap, it should avoid the schedule slippage and weather risks I've identified.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It seems we have successfully mapped the minefield. We require a project that is indoor, shared, and equipment-driven, provided it doesn't assume a budget from a decade ago or a staff of ghosts. I look forward to the candidates.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
