54,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Candidate A and D both offer shared utility. However, B is on the ground floor of an old block, and C is outdoor work; both are too risky. I vote for D.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,3,1,talk,Victoria,,"Candidate D addresses the demand for shared space and avoids the ground-floor risks Toshio mentioned. Most importantly, it is a focused fit-out that is less likely to cause the massive timetable displacement and off-site hiring costs associated with A. I vote for D.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
56,3,1,talk,George,,Candidate A is the only option that directly addresses the manual burden on our technicians by upgrading the extraction equipment. D is a luxury; A is a necessity for our staff. I vote for A.,"{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
57,3,1,talk,Mizuki,,"Candidate A is a necessity based on inspection and safety, which my research shows are the only projects delivered on time. D is an ""appeal"" project, which risks severe schedule slippage. I vote for A.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
58,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Candidate A is a necessity, but it involves extensive mechanical and ducted plant work, which I found to be the most volatile in terms of cost. Candidate D, while perhaps a ""luxury,"" is a fit-out that avoids those specific financial traps. I vote for D.","{""words"":45,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
59,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@George, @Mizuki, while A is necessary, the mechanical complexity and potential for disruption are high. D provides a shared resource that is highly valued by users and avoids the hazardous material risks of the ground floor.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
60,3,2,talk,Victoria,,"@George, @Mizuki, we must consider the scale of disruption. A ventilation upgrade across shared labs would likely take multiple rooms out of service simultaneously, triggering the expensive off-site hiring I found. D is a more contained intervention.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""George"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,2,talk,George,,"@Victoria, the cost of off-site hiring is a temporary financial burden, but the burnout of our technicians is a permanent loss. We cannot continue to ignore the manual routines they are forced to perform.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,2,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I empathize with the staff, but a project that slips by a year due to its ""appeal"" or complexity helps no one. A is an inspection-driven necessity, making it the most reliable for the calendar.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Mizuki, while I admire your faith in the calendar, we must remember that a project delivered on time but at a cost twenty percent over budget is still a failure. Candidate A is a financial gamble.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"@George, @Mizuki, the risk of a budget overrun or a schedule collapse on A is too high. D is a shared resource, which my research shows is the most successful model for user satisfaction. I remain with D.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""George"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,3,talk,Victoria,,"@George, @Mizuki, the risk of displacing our core teaching labs is too great. If we lose those rooms, the operational cost and academic disruption are immense. Candidate D provides a high-demand resource with far lower risk. I stay with D.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[""George"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,3,talk,George,,"@Toshio, @Victoria, you speak of risks and satisfaction, but you overlook the human element. A ""high-demand resource"" like D does nothing for the people keeping this campus running. I will not budge on A.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Toshio"",""Victoria""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,3,talk,Mizuki,,"@George, I understand your position, but we must be pragmatic. Candidate D is a fit-out, whereas A involves complex plant work. Given the current climate, A is the only one grounded in necessity, ensuring it actually gets finished. I vote A.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@George, @Mizuki, your passion for the staff is commendable, but we cannot ignore the lack of a renewal reserve for the very equipment A seeks to replace. We would be building on a financial void. D is the only logical choice.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""George"",""Mizuki""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,4,talk,Toshio,,"The logic is consistent. Candidate D avoids the ground-floor hazards, the weather risks, and the financial volatility of plant work, while maximizing user satisfaction through shared access. I maintain my vote for D.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,4,talk,Victoria,,The evidence is clear. Candidate D provides a high-utility shared space without the catastrophic timetable displacement and off-site costs that Candidate A would trigger. It is the most rational and sustainable choice. I maintain my vote for D.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,4,talk,George,,I cannot in good conscience support a project that ignores the exhaustion of our staff. Candidate A is the only plan that provides actual relief to our technicians. I maintain my vote for A.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,4,talk,Mizuki,,"While I care deeply for the staff, we cannot ignore the data. Candidate D is a fit-out, whereas A's mechanical complexity risks the very delays and budget slips I've seen. I'll switch to D for stability. I vote D.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears the tide of reason has finally turned. By choosing Candidate D, we avoid the financial volatility of mechanical plant work and the operational chaos of lab closures. It is the only choice that doesn't gamble with our budget. I vote D.","{""words"":43,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
